# The picture M03 is built around. A row is fixed width, so nothing of variable length can be
# inside it, and every name, signature and literal is an offset into a heap instead.
#
# The top strip is a MethodDef row because it is the row that reaches into the most heaps. The
# ldstr box is underneath rather than beside them, because #US is the one heap no row points at.

title What a metadata row actually holds

size 980 720

strip row 40 70 150 90 "RVA|where the IL is" "ImplFlags|2 bytes" "Flags|2 bytes" "Name|an offset" "Signature|an offset" "ParamList|a row number"

strip heap 40 300 180 110 "#~|the tables|fixed width rows" "#Strings|names|UTF-8, null ended" "#US|your literals|UTF-16, length first" "#Blob|signatures|length first" "#GUID|16 bytes each|counts from 1"

box ldstr 380 470 220 80 "ldstr 0x70000001" "an instruction reaches this" "heap, no row does"

arrow row.3 heap.1
arrow row.4 heap.3
arrow ldstr heap.2

note why 40 600 900 70 "A row has to be a fixed width, because finding row 400 has to be arithmetic rather than a search." "So a row holds numbers, and everything that varies in length lives in a heap with a number pointing at it."
